---
/**
 * About.
 *
 * ⚠️ This page shipped as a skeleton: three empty sections and the sentence
 * "Ring Size Tool is a free tool that ." rendered to visitors. It is the page
 * a reader opens to decide whether to trust the numbers, so it says what the
 * method is AND where it is weakest — the second half is what makes the first
 * half believable.
 *
 * Numbers are derived, same rule as everywhere else on this site.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, NOINDEX_SITE } from '../config';
import { diameterFromUs, ISO_MIN, ISO_MAX, JP_MAX } from '../data/ringSizes';

const quarterMm = diameterFromUs(6.25) - diameterFromUs(6);
const CARD_SHORT_MM = 53.98;   // ISO/IEC 7810 ID-1
---
<Layout
  title={`About — ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description={`Who builds ${SITE_NAME}, how the sizes are calculated from the published standards rather than copied from other charts, and where the method is least accurate.`}
  noindex={NOINDEX_SITE}
>
  <Prose title="About">
    <p>
      {SITE_NAME} is a free ring sizer that measures on your own screen, by first calibrating
      against a bank card so it knows how large a millimetre actually is on your display.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it exists</h2>
    <p>
      Because the ring size charts already online disagree with each other, and several disagree
      with themselves. While researching this, four well-known sizing pages gave three different
      UK answers for the same US size. One site&rsquo;s chart page and its own downloadable PDF
      printed different Japanese sizes for the same measurement. Another states that a quarter US
      size is 0.4 mm of diameter — those are the half-size figures; a quarter size is {quarterMm.toFixed(4)} mm.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of that is malice. It is what happens when charts are copied from other charts for long
      enough. So nothing here is copied. Every value is computed from the published standard —
      ISO 8653 for EU sizes, defined from {ISO_MIN} to {ISO_MAX} mm of inner circumference;
      JIS S 4700 for Japan, 1 to {JP_MAX}; BS EN 28653 for UK letters. Each of those calculations
      has assertions in the repository that fail the build if a number drifts, and the site cannot
      deploy while they fail.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where no standard exists — the US scale is a trade convention, and India has no standards
      body defining ring sizes at all — the page says so rather than presenting a number as
      settled.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
      A screen has no fixed relationship between pixels and millimetres: the same image is
      physically larger on a monitor than a laptop, and different again at 110% browser zoom. So
      the tool asks you to match an outline to a bank card, which is manufactured to
      ISO/IEC 7810 ID-1 at 85.60 × {CARD_SHORT_MM} mm worldwide. From that one match it knows your
      display&rsquo;s true scale, and everything it draws afterwards is sized from it.
    </p>
    <p>
      You then either lay a ring you own on the calibrated circle, or measure your finger with a
      strip of paper and type the length in. Both produce an inner diameter, which is the only
      measurement every national size system is actually built from.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it is less accurate</h2>
    <p>
      The arithmetic is exact; the inputs are not. A quarter US size is {quarterMm.toFixed(4)} mm
      of diameter, so a slightly loose calibration, a ring sitting at an angle on the glass, or a
      paper strip pulled half a millimetre tight will all move the result. Fingers also swell and
      shrink through the day by roughly half a size.
    </p>
    <p>
      So: measure more than once, and measure in the evening. If the answer lands between two
      sizes and the ring cannot be resized, a jeweller with a steel mandrel is more reliable than
      any screen, including this one. This tool is built to get you close enough to buy with
      confidence, not to replace a physical gauge.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions or corrections: <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>. If a number here looks wrong,
      that is the most useful message we can receive — tell us what you measured and what the tool
      returned.
    </p>
  </Prose>
</Layout>
